Title: "Refines"
Id: "c195a927-5e66-43b0-9beb-3baa0acb6735"

Text: "```mlg-fragment
[RefinedCommandHeader]
Refines: <RefinedDeclarationStatement>
implicitly?:
explicitly?:
using?: <DeclarationStatement>+
when?: <ClauseUnion>+
specifies?: <RefinedDeclarationStatement>
satisfies?: <ClauseUnion>+
Requires?: <RequiresItemUnion>+
Enables?: <EnablesItemUnion>+
Documented?: <DocumentedItemUnion>+
Justification?: <HaveGroup>+
Aliases?: <AliasItemUnion>+
Writing?: \"<WritingAlias>\"+
References?: <ResourceHeader>+
Metadata?: <MetadataItemUnion>+
Id?: <OpenText>
```

Only `Refines:` entries may use a refined command header such as:

```mlg-fragment
[\(injective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}]
Refines: f(x__)
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. adjective: \"injective\"
Id: \"23e4567e-e89b-42d3-a456-426614174000\"
```

The heading may also be a refined **specification-infix** header, for example
`[A \:(nonempty)::subset:/ B]`.

The `Refines:` section contains only the refined subject form; it does not
repeat `is <base>`. The base construct is already present after `::` in the
header, and the renderer makes it explicit — `[\(finite)::group]` with
`Refines: G ::= (X, *, e)` displays as `G ::= (X, *, e) is \group`.

A target symbol the refined base type already declares counts as specified, so
`\(associative)::binary.operation:on{X}` need not respecify the `*` that
`\binary.operation:on{X}` already types.

The target may repeat the base `Declares:` target in full or omit trailing
detail it does not need. For a base `f(x__) ::= y_`, valid refinement targets
include `f(x__) ::= y_`, `f(x__)`, and `f`. Every shape that is present must
agree with the base.

`specifies:` may state that one refinement implies other refinements, including
dynamic target syntax such as `\(injective, surjective)::[[f]]`.

`Documented:` for a `Refines:` item requires `adjective:` and may also include
`written:`, but it may not use `called:`."
Id: "6a2cef98-ca86-4edd-a8b6-133474ecb68b"

SectionTitle: "Refinement Markers"
Id: "f63d0a40-0ce3-4cf8-9c0c-a55707580518"

Text: "A refinement of a base type is automatically available on that type's subtypes:
if `\(finite)::magma` refines `\magma` and `\group` extends `\magma`, then
`\(finite)::group` is already implied. Authors may still write that refinement
out — for documentation, or to give it extra properties. Two optional, mutually
exclusive, zero-argument marker sections placed immediately after `Refines:`
state the intent, and the checker verifies the body matches the marker.

```mlg-fragment
[\(finite)::group]
Refines: G(x__)
implicitly:
specifies: G is \(finite)::magma
Documented:
. adjective: \"finite\"
```

- `implicitly:` — the group merely restates the inherited definition. The body
  must contain **nothing beyond** the inherited `specifies:` clause (the
  scaffolding `using:`/`when:` sections are still allowed); adding
  `satisfies:`, `Requires:`, `Enables:`, or `Justification:` is an error.
  The `specifies:` clause must also literally name the parent type's refinement —
  the same adjective(s) applied to a supertype of the refined base type.
- `explicitly:` — the group overrides the inherited definition with stronger
  meaning, so it must add **at least one** property beyond the inherited
  `specifies:` clause. A body that is only the inherited `specifies:` is the trivial
  case and must be marked `implicitly:` instead.

Both markers are only meaningful when the refined base type is itself a subtype
of another type. Using either on a base that is not a subtype of anything is an
error, and when the base is not a subtype no marker is written at all."
Id: "1eaeb457-2c21-4546-a529-e8e669b1de92"
